1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:02,800
The gloss app is a window on your Mac.

2
00:00:03,040 --> 00:00:08,100
But a build server has no window, and a teammate may want to read the interface

3
00:00:08,100 --> 00:00:09,320
without installing anything.

4
00:00:10,070 --> 00:00:11,970
Gloss Page is for them.

5
00:00:12,350 --> 00:00:19,150
It renders the arranged interface as one self-contained web page, for continuous integration and for sharing.

6
00:00:19,900 --> 00:00:24,000
It draws from the same Atlas as the app, so the two never disagree.

7
00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:29,800
The page loads nothing from the network, and it stamps no time.

8
00:00:30,410 --> 00:00:33,810
So the same sources always render the same bytes.

9
00:00:34,150 --> 00:00:38,970
The tests check exactly that: render twice, and the pages must be equal.

10
00:00:39,720 --> 00:00:41,860
That matters in continuous integration.

11
00:00:42,220 --> 00:00:46,800
A saved page diffs cleanly, and a change in the page means a change in the

12
00:00:46,800 --> 00:00:47,060
code.

13
00:00:49,067 --> 00:00:55,067
Writing every contract out as markup would make a large codebase's page larger than a browser

14
00:00:55,067 --> 00:00:55,987
opens comfortably.

15
00:00:56,737 --> 00:01:00,417
So the page embeds its data once, as compact JSON.

16
00:01:00,977 --> 00:01:05,257
When a reader reaches a type, the page's script builds that type's view from it.

17
00:01:05,977 --> 00:01:10,717
Everything that takes judgment is decided before that, so the script only fills templates.

18
00:01:11,467 --> 00:01:13,927
The page shows only what the model holds.

19
00:01:14,187 --> 00:01:16,727
A gap on the page is a gap in the model.

20
00:01:17,477 --> 00:01:22,117
Next, it is shrinking toward a pull request's report of findings and changes.
